2,366,600,92,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,47822,72,488,502,38154,59,60,2870,279,2629,374,4429,916,678,6785,25780,400,72,3,323,400,73,3,1741,429,400,16,1124,273,502,366,600,2418,271,37314,9960,510,27,37420,91339,397,1249,11625,279,2661,2629,1124,11520,1242,15159,16,1124,273,502,366,600,92,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,47822,72,488,502,3417,59,701,582,1184,311,1438,432,1495,3019,553,3019,4192,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nCompute\n\\[\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}},\\]where the sum is taken over all positive integers $i$ and $j$ such that $1 \\le j < i.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the given sum \\(\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}}\\), we need to break it down step by step.\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e325d3ac7fab21134dfa88bee76ff62f778beb483cf241b802f9be946c7cc01b"},"seconds":56.558308601379395,"seed":1142866605254990725,"stop":"eos","text":"First, we observe that the sum is over all pairs \\((i, j)\\) such that \\(1 \\le j < i\\). This means for each \\(i\\), \\(j\\) can take values from \\(1\\) to \\(i-1\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the sum as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's focus on the inner sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}} = \\frac{1}{2^i} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\n\\]\n\nThe inner sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\\) is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\). The sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric series is given by \\(\\frac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}\\). Therefore, for \\(n = i-1\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{i-1})}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into our expression for the inner sum, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2^i} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can substitute this back into the original sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\left(\\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWe can split this into two separate sums:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\n\\]\n\nThe first sum is a geometric series starting from \\(i=2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe second sum can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{4^{i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{4^k} \\quad \\text{where } k = i-1\n\\]\n\nThis is again a geometric series:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{4^k} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{3}{4}} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, the second sum is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"First, we observe that the sum is over all pairs \\((i, j)\\) such that \\(1 \\le j < i\\). This means for each \\(i\\), \\(j\\) can take values from \\(1\\) to \\(i-1\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the sum as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's focus on the inner sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}} = \\frac{1}{2^i} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\n\\]\n\nThe inner sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\\) is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\). The sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric series is given by \\(\\frac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}\\). Therefore, for \\(n = i-1\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{i-1})}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into our expression for the inner sum, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2^i} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can substitute this back into the original sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\left(\\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWe can split this into two separate sums:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\n\\]\n\nThe first sum is a geometric series starting from \\(i=2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe second sum can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{4^{i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{4^k} \\quad \\text{where } k = i-1\n\\]\n\nThis is again a geometric series:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{4^k} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{3}{4}} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, the second sum is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:141"}
